{"builder":"Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so that tab-separated input on stdin becomes the same JSON array the CLI already prints for the same table as CSV. Document the flag in README.md. Everything goes in one pull request.\n\nContext: bin/csv2json.js reads stdin and prints `JSON.stringify(parse(input))` followed by a newline. src/parse.js already exports `parse(text, sep = ',')`, so it can split on any separator. test/csv.test.js holds a parse test and a CLI test, and `npm test` runs `node --test`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv (after the script path) contains `--tsv`. If it does, call `parse(input, '\\t')`. If it does not, call `parse(input)` exactly as now. Do not change the output format: still JSON.stringify of the array plus one trailing newline. Plain CSV behaviour must not change at all.\n2. Leave src/parse.js as it is unless you actually need to change it. If you change it, make sure the existing tests still pass.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a CLI test that runs `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` with input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n'. Assert that its stdout equals the stdout of `node bin/csv2json.js` given 'a,b\\n1,2\\n', which is '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'. Also add a test with several rows and columns.\n4. In README.md, document --tsv. Add a usage example such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and one sentence saying that the flag reads tab-separated input instead of CSV.\n5. Run `npm test` and make sure every test passes. Then run both commands by hand on the same small table, once as TSV with --tsv and once as CSV without the flag, and confirm the outputs are byte-identical.\n6. Open the pull request with these changes.","verifier":"Run every check at the pull request's head, from the repository root.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun:\n  printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nAnn\\t30\\tOslo\\nBob\\t41\\tRome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/tsv.out; echo \"exit $?\"\n  printf 'name,age,city\\nAnn,30,Oslo\\nBob,41,Rome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/csv.out; echo \"exit $?\"\n  cmp /tmp/tsv.out /tmp/csv.out && cat /tmp/tsv.out\nRepeat with a second table, a\\tb / 1\\t2 against a,b / 1,2.\nPASS: both commands exit 0, cmp reports no difference for both tables, and the output is a JSON array of objects keyed by the header row (for the first table, [{\"name\":\"Ann\",\"age\":\"30\",\"city\":\"Oslo\"},{\"name\":\"Bob\",\"age\":\"41\",\"city\":\"Rome\"}]).\nFAIL: either command exits non-zero, the outputs differ for either table, or the --tsv output is not that JSON array (for example, a single key holding the whole tab-joined line).\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRun: grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md, then read README.md at the head.\nPASS: README.md mentions --tsv and says it is used for tab-separated input, for example in a usage line or a sentence.\nFAIL: README.md does not mention --tsv, or mentions it without saying what it does.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail):\n- Run `npm test` at the head and note any failures.\n- Check that plain CSV output without --tsv is unchanged: printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js should print [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}].\n- Note whether a test for --tsv was added."}